<h2 id='options-web-sockets'>Web Sockets</h2>

WebSockets are used to provide real-time updates and maintain a continuous connection between clients (traders) and servers (exchanges).

Here's how different web sockets work:


<h3 id="options-authenticated-web-sockets">Authenticated Web Sockets</h3>

Authenticated WebSockets require user validation to ensure that only authorized users can access the data or functionality provided such as receiving real-time updates about a trader's account.

For example,  balance changes, order executions, or margin updates.

<aside class="notice"> Notes:
<ul>
  <li>Authenticated Web Socket stream base URL <code>https://fawss-uds-options.pi42.com/auth-stream </code></li>
  <li>Requires listenkey that can be obtained from the <a href="#options-create-listen-key">Create Listen Key API</a></li>
</ul>
</aside>

The WebSocket client connects to a server using a listen key and listens for various events related to trading activities and session management. Each event handler logs specific information when the event is received.

Event listeners are described below:


```javascript
const serverUrl = `https://fawss-uds-options.pi42.com/auth-stream/${listenKey}`;

const socket = io(serverUrl);

socket.on('connect', () => {
  console.log('Connected to WebSocket server');
});

socket.on('order', (data) => {
  console.log('New order:', data);
});

socket.on('trade', (data) => {
  console.log('Trade executed:', data);
});

socket.on('greeks', (data) => {
  console.log('Option Greeks updated:', data);
});

socket.on('wallet', (data) => {
  console.log('Wallet balance/margin updated:', data);
});

socket.on('position', (data) => {
  console.log('Position updated:', data);
});

socket.on('orderFailed', (data) => {
  console.log('Order failed:', data);
});

socket.on('fnfWallet', (data) => {
  console.log('Funds moved in/out of wallet:', data);
});

socket.on('disconnect', () => {
  console.log('Disconnected from WebSocket server');
});
```
```python
# Required installations:
# python-socketio asyncio aiohttp

import socketio
import asyncio

# URL of the WebSocket server (replace with actual authenticated URL)
server_url = 'Pi42-Authenticated-websocket-url'

# Namespace path including your listen key for authenticated streaming
namespace_path = '/auth-stream/your-listen-key'

# Create a new asynchronous Socket.IO client instance
sio = socketio.AsyncClient()


# Event handler called upon successful connection to the WebSocket server
@sio.event
async def connect():
    print('Successfully connected to the WebSocket server')


# Event handler called when the connection to the WebSocket server is closed
@sio.event
async def disconnect():
    print('Disconnected from the WebSocket server')


# Handles 'order' events when a new order is created or updated
@sio.on('order', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_order(data):
    print("New order:", data)


# Handles 'trade' events when a trade is executed
@sio.on('trade', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_trade(data):
    print("Trade executed:", data)


# Handles 'greeks' events when option Greeks (delta, gamma, etc.) data is updated
@sio.on('greeks', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_greeks(data):
    print("Option Greeks updated:", data)


# Handles 'wallet' events when wallet balance or margin changes
@sio.on('wallet', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_wallet(data):
    print("Wallet balance/margin updated:", data)


# Handles 'position' events when an open position is created, modified, or closed
@sio.on('position', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_position(data):
    print("Position updated:", data)


# Handles 'orderFailed' events when an order fails to process
@sio.on('orderFailed', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_order_failed(data):
    print("Order failed:", data)


# Handles 'fnfWallet' events when funds move in/out due to fees, funding, or settlements
@sio.on('fnfWallet', namespace=namespace_path)
async def on_fnf_wallet(data):
    print("Funds moved in/out of wallet:", data)


# Event handler called if the connection attempt fails
@sio.event
async def connect_error(data):
    print('Failed to connect to the WebSocket server:', data)


# Main coroutine that connects and keeps the connection alive to listen for events
async def main():
    try:
        await sio.connect(server_url, transports=["websocket"])
        await sio.wait()  # Keeps the program running to listen indefinitely
    except Exception as e:
        print("Connection error:", e)


# Entry point for the async program
if __name__ == '__main__':
    asyncio.run(main())
```
